y the hypothesis and give a counterexample","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_h120","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h120","cnm_071","cnm_064","cnm_067","cnm_h115","math:line-plane-perpendicular-definition"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:921578b27047c0dab62684d0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3:1","reason":"Let AD be parallel to CP with D on BC. The bisector and parallel-angle relations give CD=CA. Taking CA=3t and CB=4t gives BD=t, so BA/BP=BD/BC=1/4. Therefore PA=BP-BA=3AB.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_067"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_055","cnm_268","cnm_067"],"id":"parallel-bisector-construction","label":"Draw a parallel to the bisector","methodDirectNodeIds":{"parallel-bisector-construction":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_269"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_269","cnm_058","cnm_055","cnm_268","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:921578b27047c0dab62684d0"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D. (1) or (2) only","reason":"The vectors OP and QR are equal, as are OQ and PR. Noncollinear P and Q therefore give a parallelogram; collinear position vectors give a straight-line figure.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_067"],"coverage":"aligned_concept_only","flags":["Uses the exclusive definition of trapezoid: exactly one pair of parallel sides. An inclusive definition would change the choice.","The answer is D when a trapezoid has exactly one pair of parallel sides. With the inclusive definition, parallelograms are also trapezoids and E would apply."],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_082","cnm_067","cnm_053"],"id":"coordinate-translations","label":"Compare opposite sides using translations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coordinate-transla